{"moat":{"rating":"narrow","sources_of_moat":["Global brand and the Jordan/Swoosh franchises. Jordan is still 13% of the business, even though it is shrinking.","Scale: about $46B of revenue, a 42.8% gross margin, and a large athlete and sports marketing budget that smaller rivals can't match.","Innovation platforms (Air, ZoomX, Vomero and max-cushion running). Performance revenue is about $16B and is growing.","Distribution reach across wholesale and owned channels, including Nike Direct at $4.1B per quarter. Direct is currently shrinking because of the China digital reset.","Reputation for being credible in performance sport. This is stronger in performance categories than in Sportswear."],"evidence":"The moat is real but eroding, so I rate it narrow rather than wide. Operating margin fell from 12.3% (FY24) to about 8.2% (FY26), revenue fell about 10% from FY24 to FY26, and FCF dropped from $6.6B to $2.2B (user-provided market data). Q1 FY27 revenue fell 4%, and management guides FY27 revenue down by a high-single-digit percentage. Sportswear fell low double digits, Jordan fell mid-teens, and Greater China fell 26% (cite 1-4, 1-6, 2-6, 2-7, 9-14). Nike cut Dunk sales by about 50% on purpose (cite 1-4). Promotions and discounts have gone up, and analysts blame a lack of compelling new product (cite 24-5). That points to weaker pricing power in lifestyle. Third-party sources say Nike's share of the global sportswear market is roughly 27% and the lead is narrowing, with Adidas at about 20% and On and Hoka growing at double-digit rates (cite 13-1, 13-2). Interbrand cut Nike's brand value by 25.9% (cite 16-1). The evidence in favor: Performance grew high single digits in Q1, and gross margin rose 60bp to 42.8% (cite 4-3, 4-10). Running grew at a double-digit rate and Nike nearly tripled its max-cushion share (cite 1-8, 1-9). Brand strength remains, but returns on capital have fallen. Whether the margin damage is cyclical or structural is still unproven."},"management":{"rating":"average","capital_allocation":"Capital allocation has been mixed. Nike has repurchased about $12.1B of stock at an average of $97.57, and the stock now trades near $33 (cite 26-8). That has destroyed value. Q1 FY27 buybacks were $0, against $126M a year earlier, and the company paid $610M in dividends, so FCF was about -$64M (user-provided 10-Q data). The dividend was raised to $0.41 per share. Dividends of about $2.4B a year now exceed FY26 FCF of $2.2B, so the payout is not covered by free cash flow at current levels. This is a sustainability concern if FCF does not recover (computed from provided data). The balance sheet is sound, with $8.4B in cash and short-term investments and a net cash position (cite 2-10). Management launched the 'Pace' productivity program. It targets about $2.5B in savings against about $1B in implementation costs, plus about $300M of severance in FY26 (cite 1-5, 4-16). Q1 SG&A fell 3% while demand creation spending rose (cite 4-19). CEO Elliott Hill, a Nike veteran, returned in Oct 2024. He has refocused the company on sport and is rebuilding wholesale relationships (cite 24-4). Execution has disappointed so far: the turnaround is slower than expected, and he conceded as much in June (cite 21-1, 21-2). The new CFO is Dave Denton (cite 1-13). FY27 guidance is for adjusted EPS of $1.15-$1.35, excluding about $0.15 of Pace costs (cite 9-16). Bloomberg commentary argues the reset came too late and may still be too little (cite 25-2:25-4). Further updates are due from November (cite 24-8).","governance_concerns":["Dual-class structure. Phil Knight's entity controls most Class A shares and board seats, so activist and proxy-fight pressure is hard to apply (cite 26-6). A shareholder proposal to sunset the dual-class structure was opposed by the company (cite 26-2).","Buybacks at much higher prices (average $97.57 versus a share price near $33) point to weak capital discipline under the prior regime (cite 26-8).","Turnaround credibility. Hill admitted to staff and investors that progress has been slower than hoped (cite 21-2, 22-5:22-6). Fortune points to key-person dependence on Hill (cite 23-10).","Reduced transparency on women's reporting was flagged (cite 22-1).","Repeated guidance resets. FY27 guidance came in as a surprisingly steep revenue decline (cite 24-2).","Nike lost its S&P 100 seat after a roughly 78% share-price fall, which is a symbolic comedown for the stock (cite 19-3, 19-4).","All 11 directors were re-elected at the annual meeting, so there is no sign of board turnover or shareholder pushback in the vote (cite 20-8)."]},"industry":{"growth_outlook":"The global sportswear market was about $334B in 2025 (cite 13-3). It is large and growing, and another source puts it near $400B (cite 17-6). The industry itself is not shrinking. Nike's problem is share loss and its own reset, not a collapsing end market. Near term, Nike guides FY27 revenue down by a high-single-digit percentage, partly from deliberate supply cuts in the marketplace (cite 3-4). Greater China is expected to worsen over the rest of FY27 (cite 9-15). Long-term industry growth forecasts are unknown from the sources I found. I am not giving a specific number.","competitive_intensity":"High and rising. Competition is segmented. Adidas competes across all categories and has revived through Samba and Gazelle (cite 16-1, 10-3). On and Hoka are specialist running challengers with premium pricing and full-price sell-through, and they have expanded into wholesale doors Nike de-prioritised (cite 10-9). New Balance and ASICS are also gaining ground (cite 14-7). In China, local brands Anta and Li-Ning have strengthened (cite 10-1). Lululemon competes in athleisure.","key_competitors":["Adidas","On Holding","Deckers (Hoka)","New Balance","ASICS","Puma","Anta","Li-Ning","Lululemon","Under Armour","Skechers"]},"catalysts":["Pace program: about $2.5B of savings, with the cost plan and operating-model updates due from the November investor day / target update (cite 1-5, 24-8, 21-8).","Performance categories (running, football, basketball, tennis, golf) are growing high single digits and could offset Sportswear and Jordan declines. They were growing low double digits excluding the China reset (cite 4-10).","The gross margin rose 60bp to 42.8%, and the Dunk and Jordan retro supply cuts are a reset. Once they finish, lifestyle revenue may stabilise and discounts could fall (cite 3-8, 4-3).","China digital cleanup and made-for-China collections. Nike will pull online sales rights from some retail partners from January, and the first China-made-for-China collection launches in October. The near-term hit is large, but a later recovery is possible (cite 24-6, 5-4).","North America growth of 2%, with good sell-through across channels, and wholesale relationships being rebuilt (cite 2-13, 24-4).","Management says the full impact of the restructuring should show early next year as new products roll out (cite 21-5).","Valuation. The stock is down about 78% from its peak and trades near $33, with a trailing P/E of 15.75x on depressed earnings. A margin recovery toward the 12% level would lift earnings and FCF materially. Nothing provided confirms this recovery (user-provided data; cite 19-2).","Risks to watch: the FY27 EPS guide of $1.15-$1.35 implies a sharp earnings drop, and the dividend is not covered by FCF."],"citations":["https://finance.yahoo.com/markets/stocks/articles/nike-q1-earnings-call-highlights-000223968.html","https://finance.yahoo.com/markets/stocks/articles/nike-inc-nke-q1-2027-050050135.html","https://www.fool.com/earnings/call-transcripts/2026/10/02/nike-nke-q1-2027-earnings-call-transcript/","https://ca.investing.com/news/company-news/nike-inc-nke-q1-2027-earnings-call-highlights-performance-gains-offset-by-china-reset-and--4863264","https://gloom.sh/stocks/nke/transcripts/q1-2027","https://www.tradingkey.com/news/transcripts/262197031-tradingkey","https://www.businessessays.ai/business-strategy/a-competitor-analysis-of-nike-in-the-global-sportswear-market/","https://www.companieshistory.com/nike-competitors/","https://pestel-analysis.com/blogs/competitors/nike","https://businessmodelanalyst.com/nike-swot-analysis/","https://thedutchinvestors.substack.com/p/nike-premium-research-report-part","https://wwd.com/footwear-news/shoe-industry-news/nike-annual-shareholder-meeting-ceo-elliott-hill-progress-1239201228/","https://finance.yahoo.com/markets/stocks/articles/nike-elliott-hill-aims-squash-205335634.html","https://www.benzinga.com/markets/equities/26/06/60007561/nike-ceo-elliott-hill-admits-turnaround-is-taking-longer-than-expected-we-still-have-work-to-do","https://fortune.com/2026/07/04/nike-earnings-elliott-hill-nascent-comeback/","https://fortune.com/article/nike-ceo-veteran-elliott-hill-turnaround-growth/","https://www.spokesman.com/stories/2026/oct/04/nike-plans-more-job-cuts-to-boost-sputtering-turna/","https://bloomberg.com/opinion/articles/2026-10-05/nike-s-other-shoe-has-finally-dropped","https://www.forbes.com/sites/jimosman/2026/09/08/nike-stock-is-ripe-for-an-activist-but-theres-one-big-problem/"]}
