 "bear_case": [
  "The base-case upside of 41.9% comes from a $4.023B starting FCF. That figure is the FY24–FY26 three-year average, (6.617+3.268+2.184)/3. It is 84% above the latest FCF of $2.184B and includes the FY24 peak. Scaling the $46.95 base value to FY26 FCF gives about $25–26 per share, roughly 23% below the $33.09 price. The model's headline conclusion is an artifact of the normalization choice.",
  "Earnings are falling, not recovering. FY27 adjusted EPS is guided to $1.15–$1.35, and that excludes about $0.15 of Pace costs. Trailing EPS is about $2.09–$2.10, so the guide implies a drop of roughly 35–45%. At $33.09 the stock trades at about 25–29x guided EPS, not 15.75x. The 'cheap on trailing P/E' framing rests on earnings that management says are going away.",
  "Management guides FY27 revenue down by a high-single-digit percent. Q1 revenue was down 4.3%, Greater China was down 26% and is expected to worsen, Sportswear fell low double digits and Jordan fell mid-teens. The base case's +3% FCF growth and +3% terminal growth sit against a shrinking top line, shrinking lifestyle franchises and deliberate supply cuts (Dunk down about 50%).",
  "The dividend is not covered. Q1 dividends were $610M, or about $2.4B annualized, against FY26 FCF of $2.18B and Q1 FCF of about -$64M. Cash fell $660M in one quarter, and buybacks were paused. If the earnings guide holds, a cut or debt-funded payout is plausible. Management cannot both fund Pace restructuring and maintain a 112%-of-FCF dividend.",
  "Cash conversion is deteriorating. FCF/net income went from 116% to 102% to 70%. Operating cash flow fell 61% from FY24 to FY26 while operating income fell 40%. Q1 operating cash flow of $135M compared with net income of $712M, with a $1.2B outflow from payables and accruals. That looks like a working-capital unwind and may repeat.",
  "Inventory rose 4.6% in the quarter while revenue fell 4.3%. Combined with a deliberate cut of Dunk and Jordan retro supply, this raises markdown risk. The gross margin gain (+60bp) may not last if promotions rise. The 10-Q gives no inventory aging data.",
  "Competitive share loss looks structural. On, Hoka, Adidas (Samba and Gazelle), New Balance, ASICS and local Chinese brands (Anta, Li-Ning) are taking share in the categories and wholesale doors Nike de-prioritised. Interbrand cut Nike's brand value by 25.9%. Nike has also lost pricing power in lifestyle. The 'reset' may not simply reverse once supply cuts end.",
  "Restructuring is ongoing and recurring. Pace targets about $2.5B in savings for about $1B in costs, plus about $300M of FY26 severance and more job cuts reported in October. Excluding about $0.15 per share of Pace costs from adjusted EPS flatters earnings. Cost savings are being partly reinvested in demand creation, which rose 5.4%, so little may reach the bottom line.",
  "FY26 earnings quality is weak. Operating income rose 2.6% on flat revenue, yet net income fell 3.5% and operating cash flow fell 22%. Q1 pre-tax income held up only because of a $42M swing in other income/expense (about 4.6% of pre-tax income), while underlying operating profit fell to $888M from $927M. The effective tax rate also rose to 22.7%.",
  "The balance sheet is less strong than 'net cash' suggests. Net cash excluding leases is only about $475M. Including the $3.2B of lease liabilities, net debt is about $2.8B. $2.0B of debt is current, including bonds due Nov 2026 and Mar 2027 and a credit facility maturing Mar 2027. Refinancing at higher rates would add interest expense while net interest income is already falling.",
  "Governance is a problem for a turnaround. The dual-class structure leaves little shareholder leverage, and the company opposed a proposal to sunset it. Buybacks of about $12.1B at an average of $97.57 destroyed value. Guidance has been reset repeatedly, the CEO has admitted the turnaround is slower than hoped, and there is key-person risk around Hill."
 ],
