totalCash"},{"metric":"Financial debt (current + long-term)","value":"$2,000M + $5,893M = $7,893M","period":"Aug 31, 2026","source":"SEC 10-Q (2026-10-02)"},{"metric":"Operating lease liabilities","value":"$473M current + $2,706M non-current","period":"Aug 31, 2026","source":"SEC 10-Q (2026-10-02)"},{"metric":"Inventories","value":"$7,846M vs $7,501M at May 31, 2026 (+4.6% computed)","period":"Aug 31, 2026","source":"SEC 10-Q (2026-10-02)"},{"metric":"Shareholders' equity","value":"$15,220M; retained earnings deficit of $(56)M","period":"Aug 31, 2026","source":"SEC 10-Q (2026-10-02)"},{"metric":"Shares outstanding","value":"Class A 281,387,752 + Class B 1,203,921,077 = 1,485,308,829","period":"Sep 28, 2026","source":"SEC 10-Q (2026-10-02) cover"}],"recommended_base_growth_rate":0.03,"growth_rationale":"This is an analyst judgment, not a figure from any source; no document provides guidance or consensus estimates. The evidence is mixed. FCF fell from $6.617B in FY2024 to $2.184B in FY2026, and revenue declined from $51.4B to $46.4B and was flat in FY2026 ($46.309B to $46.398B) (Market Data). Q1 FY2027 revenue fell 4.3% and FCF was about -$64M (SEC 10-Q). Operating margin dropped from 12.3% to about 8.2% (computed), so the FCF base is cyclically and structurally depressed. A partial margin recovery could lift FCF faster, but nothing provided supports that. Gross margin improved to 42.8% from 42.2% and operating overhead fell about 6% in Q1 (SEC 10-Q), a modest positive. Forward P/E (18.7x) above trailing P/E (15.75x) implies the market expects lower near-term EPS (Market Data). I therefore use a low, positive 3% FCF growth rate. Treat it as low confidence and run sensitivity cases.","growth_sources":["Market Data history (revenue, operating income, FCF, OCF, capex, net income FY2023-FY2026)","SEC 10-Q (2026-10-02): Q1 FY2027 income statement, cash flow and balance sheet","Market Data trailing/forward P/E","Yahoo Finance - Analysis page: no estimate data returned (page error / empty growth table)"],"stated_risks":["No risk-factor text (10-K Item 1A or 10-Q Part II Item 1A) is in the provided material, so no management-stated risks can be quoted. The 10-Q table of contents lists Item 1A on page 38, but its content is not provided.","Debt maturity: current portion of long-term debt is $2,000M at Aug 31, 2026 (SEC 10-Q). XBRL tags in the 10-K reference a 2.38% corporate bond due Nov 1, 2026, a 2.75% bond due Mar 27, 2027 and a committed credit facility maturing Mar 5, 2027 (SEC 10-K XBRL tags, amounts not provided). Cash and short-term investments of $8,368M exceed the $2,000M current debt (SEC 10-Q).","Disclosure topics in the 10-Q notes whose content is not provided: FX/interest-rate hedging (Note 7), commitments and contingencies (Note 11), severance and other employee costs (Note 12), supplier finance programs (Note 13) and subsequent events (Note 14), plus a 10-K segment structure that includes Greater China (SEC 10-Q table of contents; SEC 10-K XBRL tags)."],"accounting_red_flags":[{"flag":"FCF is declining faster than net income, and cash conversion is weakening","evidence":"FCF fell from $6.617B (FY24) to $3.268B (FY25) to $2.184B (FY26). FCF/net income was 116% in FY24 and 70% in FY26 (computed). OCF fell from $7.429B to $2.868B while FY26 net income was $3.108B. Q1 FY27 OCF was only $135M vs net income of $712M, with a $1,219M outflow from payables, accrued liabilities and other liabilities (SEC 10-Q).","source":"Market Data (history); SEC 10-Q (2026-10-02)"},{"flag":"Dividends are not covered by free cash flow in the latest quarter","evidence":"Q1 FY27 dividends paid were $610M against OCF of $135M and FCF of about -$64M. Cash and equivalents fell $660M to $6,903M. Buybacks were paused ($0 vs $126M). The retained earnings account is a deficit of $(56)M (SEC 10-Q).","source":"SEC 10-Q (2026-10-02)"},{"flag":"Inventory rising while revenue is falling","evidence":"Inventories were $7,846M at Aug 31, 2026 vs $7,501M at May 31, 2026 (+4.6%) while Q1 revenue fell 4.3% year over year. Inventory used $312M of cash in Q1 (vs $610M in the prior-year quarter) (SEC 10-Q). The year-over-year comparison is not available because the Aug 31, 2025 balance sheet is not provided.","source":"SEC 10-Q (2026-10-02)"},{"flag":"Non-cash capex additions understate cash capex and therefore FCF","evidence":"Non-cash additions to PP&E were $225M in Q1 FY27 vs $101M a year earlier, against cash capex of $199M. This capex will be paid in later periods, so FCF built from cash capex may overstate sustainable FCF. Capex also dropped to $430M in FY25 from $812M in FY24, then rose to $684M in FY26 (Market Data), so the FY25 FCF base may be flattered.","source":"SEC 10-Q (2026-10-02) supplemental cash flow; Market Data (history)"},{"flag":"Market-data share count conflicts with the filing","evidence":"Market Data 'shares' is 1,202,110,951, which matches Class B only. The 10-Q cover shows 1,485,308,829 total shares (Class A 281.4M + Class B 1,203.9M). Market cap of $49.08B / $33.085 implies about 1.484B shares, so market cap uses the total. Using the 'shares' field for per-share valuation would overstate value per share by about 23%.","source":"Market Data; SEC 10-Q (2026-10-02) cover"},{"flag":"Debt figure in market data includes leases and does not reconcile exactly","evidence":"Market Data totalDebt is $11.125B. Balance-sheet financial debt is $7,893M, and adding operating lease liabilities of $3,179M gives $11,072M at Aug 31, 2026, not an exact match. The difference of about $53M is unexplained from the material provided. Net cash excluding leases is about $475M (computed).","source":"Market Data; SEC 10-Q (2026-10-02)"},{"flag":"Effective tax rate increased","evidence":"Q1 FY27 tax expense of $209M on pre-tax income of $921M is 22.7%, vs $195M on $922M, or 21.1%, a year earlier (computed). Pre-tax income was flat, so net income fell while taxes rose.","source":"SEC 10-Q (2026-10-02)"},{"flag":"Other income/expense swing supports pre-tax income","evidence":"Other (income) expense, net was income of $19M vs expense of $23M a year earlier, a $42M swing, equal to about 4.6% of Q1 pre-tax income of $921M. Underlying operating profit (gross profit less selling and administrative expense, computed) was $888M vs $927M. Net interest income fell to $14M from $18M.","source":"SEC 10-Q (2026-10-02)"},{"flag":"Cost cuts vs. brand investment mix","evidence":"Operating overhead fell 6.0% ($2,658M vs $2,828M) while demand creation rose 5.4% ($1,252M vs $1,188M) and stock-based compensation fell to $158M from $185M. Revenue fell 4.3%. The sustainability of the overhead reductions is not addressed in the provided text, and the 10-Q mentions severance costs (Note 12) but its amounts are not provided.","source":"SEC 10-Q (2026-10-02)"}],"data_gaps":["The 10-K (2026-07-15) text provided is XBRL metadata only; no FY2026 financial statements, MD&A, risk factors or footnotes. FY2026 figures come solely from Market Data history.","The 10-Q MD&A, risk factors, Notes 3-14 detail (revenue by segment/channel, derivatives, severance, supplier finance, contingencies, subsequent events) and the debt schedule are truncated or not provided, so segment growth and Greater China trends cannot be assessed.","No management guidance and no analyst consensus: the Yahoo Finance Analysis page returned an error with empty growth estimate tables. Forward EPS and revenue growth estimates are null.","Quarterly detail of FY2026 and the Aug 31, 2025 balance sheet are not provided, so year-over-year working-capital comparisons are not possible.","FY2026 dividends paid and buybacks are not in the provided material. Annualizing the $0.41 quarterly dividend on about 1.485B shares gives about $2.4B (computed), which is above FY26 FCF of $2.184B, but this is an inference.","Market Data 'shares' field (Class B only) conflicts with the 10-Q total share count; the correct diluted count for valuation is about 1.484-1.485B (SEC 10-Q).","Competitor, peer valuation, tariff exposure and inventory aging are not in the material provided; these are null."]}
Qualitative: {"moat":{"rating":"narrow","sources_of_moat":["Global brand and the Jordan/Swoosh franchises. Jordan is still 13% of the business, even though it is shrinking.","Scale: about $46B of revenue, a 42.8% gross margin, and a large athlete and sports marketing budget that smaller rivals can't match.","Innovation platforms (Air, ZoomX, Vomero and max-cushion running). Performance revenue is about $16B and is growing.","Distribution reach across wholesale and owned channels, including Nike Direct at $4.1B per quarter. Direct is currently shrinking because of the China digital reset.","Reputation for being credible in performance sport. This is stronger in performance categories than in Sportswear."],"evidence":"The moat is real but eroding, so I rate it narrow rather than wide. Operating margin fell from 12.3% (FY24) to about 8.2% (FY26), revenue fell about 10% from FY24 to FY26, and FCF dropped from $6.6B to $2.2B (user-provided market data). Q1 FY27 revenue fell 4%, and management guides FY27 revenue down by a high-single-digit percentage. Sportswear fell low double digits, Jordan fell mid-teens, and Greater China fell 26% (cite 1-4, 1-6, 2-6, 2-7, 9-14). Nike cut Dunk sales by about 50% on purpose (cite 1-4). Promotions and discounts have gone up, and analysts blame a lack of compelling new product (cite 24-5). That points to weaker pricing power in lifestyle. Third-party sources say Nike's share of the global sportswear market is roughly 27% and the lead is narrowing, with Adidas at about 20% and On and Hoka growing at double-digit rates (cite 13-1, 13-2). Interbrand cut Nike's brand value by 25.9% (cite 16-1). The evidence in favor: Performance grew high single digits in Q1, and gross margin rose 60bp to 42.8% (cite 4-3, 4-10). Running grew at a double-digit rate and Nike nearly tripled its max-cushion share (cite 1-8, 1-9). Brand strength remains, but returns on capital have fallen. Whether the margin damage is cyclical or structural is still unproven."},"management":{"rating":"average","capital_allocation":"Capital allocation has been mixed. Nike has repurchased about $12.1B of stock at an average of $97.57, and the stock now trades near $33 (cite 26-8). That has destroyed value. Q1 FY27 buybacks were $0, against $126M a year earlier, and the company paid $610M in dividends, so FCF was about -$64M (user-provided 10-Q data). The dividend was raised to $0.41 per share. Dividends of about $2.4B a year now exceed FY26 FCF of $2.2B, so the payout is not covered by free cash flow at current levels. This is a sustainability concern if FCF does not recover (computed from provided data). The balance sheet is sound, with $8.4B in cash and short-term investments and a net cash position (cite 2-10). Management launched the 'Pace' productivity program. It targets about $2.5B in savings against about $1B in implementation costs, plus about $300M of severance in FY26 (cite 1-5, 4-16). Q1 SG&A fell 3% while demand creation spending rose (cite 4-19). CEO Elliott Hill, a Nike veteran, returned in Oct 2024. He has refocused the company on sport and is rebuilding wholesale relationships (cite 24-4). Execution has disappointed so far: the turnaround is slower than expected, and he conceded as much in June (cite 21-1, 21-2). The new CFO is Dave Denton (cite 1-13). FY27 guidance is for adjusted EPS of $1.15-$1.35, excluding about $0.15 of Pace costs (cite 9-16). Bloomberg commentary argues the reset came too late and may still be too little (cite 25-2:25-4). Further updates are due from November (cite 24-8).","governance_concerns":["Dual-class structure. Phil Knight's entity controls most Class A shares and board seats, so activist and proxy-fight pressure is hard to apply (cite 26-6). A shareholder proposal to sunset the dual-class structure was opposed by the company (cite 26-2).","Buybacks at much higher prices (average $97.57 versus a share price near $33) point to weak capital discipline under the prior regime (cite 26-8).","Turnaround credibility. Hill admitted to staff and investors that progress has been slower than hoped (cite 21-2, 22-5:22-6). Fortune points to key-person dependence on Hill (cite 23-10).","Reduced transparency on women's reporting was flagged (cite 22-1).","Repeated guidance resets. FY27 guidance came in as a surprisingly steep revenue decline (cite 24-2).","Nike lost its S&P 100 seat after a roughly 78% share-price fall, which is a symbolic comedown for the stock (cite 19-3, 19-4).","All 11 directors were re-elected at the annual meeting, so there is no sign of board turnover or shareholder pushback in the vote (cite 20-8)."]},"industry":{"growth_outlook":"The global sportswear market was about $334B in 2025 (cite 13-3). It is large and growing, and another source puts it near $400B (cite 17-6). The industry itself is not shrinking. Nike's problem is share loss and its own reset, not a collapsing end market. Near term, Nike guides FY27 revenue down by a high-single-digit percentage, partly from deliberate supply cuts in the marketplace (cite 3-4). Greater China is expected to worsen over the rest of FY27 (cite 9-15). Long-term industry growth forecasts are unknown from the sources I found. I am not giving a specific number.","competitive_intensity":"High and rising. Competition is segmented. Adidas competes across all categories and has revived through Samba and Gazelle (cite 16-1, 10-3). On and Hoka are specialist running challengers with premium pricing and full-price sell-through, and they have expanded into wholesale doors Nike de-prioritised (cite 10-9). New Balance and ASICS are also gaining ground (cite 14-7). In China, local brands Anta and Li-Ning have strengthened (cite 10-1). Lululemon competes in athleisure.","key_competitors":["Adidas","On Holding","Deckers (Hoka)","New Balance","ASICS","Puma","Anta","Li-Ning","Lululemon","Under Armour","Skechers"]},"catalysts":["Pace program: about $2.5B of savings, with the cost plan and operating-model updates due from the November investor day / target update (cite 1-5, 24-8, 21-8).","Performance categories (running, football, basketball, tennis, golf) are growing high single digits and could offset Sportswear and Jordan declines. They were growing low double digits excluding the China reset (cite 4-10).","The gross margin rose 60bp to 42.8%, and the Dunk and Jordan retro supply cuts are a reset. Once they finish, lifestyle revenue may stabilise and discounts could fall (cite 3-8, 4-3).","China digital cleanup and made-for-China collections. Nike will pull online sales rights from some retail partners from January, and the first China-made-for-China collection launches in October. The near-term hit is large, but a later recovery is possible (cite 24-6, 5-4).","North America growth of 2%, with good sell-through across channels, and wholesale relationships being rebuilt (cite 2-13, 24-4).","Management says the full impact of the restructuring should show early next year as new products roll out (cite 21-5).","Valuation. The stock is down about 78% from its peak and trades near $33, with a trailing P/E of 15.75x on depressed earnings. A margin recovery toward the 12% level would lift earnings and FCF materially. Nothing provided confirms this recovery (user-provided data; cite 19-2).","Risks to watch: the FY27 EPS guide of $1.15-$1.35 implies a sharp earnings drop, and the dividend is not covered by FCF."],"citations":["https://finance.yahoo.com/markets/stocks/articles/nike-q1-earnings-call-highlights-000223968.html","https://finance.yahoo.com/markets/stocks/articles/nike-inc-nke-q1-2027-050050135.html","https://www.fool.com/earnings/call-transcripts/2026/10/02/nike-nke-q1-2027-earnings-call-transcript/","https://ca.investing.com/news/company-news/nike-inc-nke-q1-2027-earnings-call-highlights-performance-gains-offset-by-china-reset-and--4863264","https://gloom.sh/stocks/nke/transcripts/q1-2027","https://www.tradingkey.com/news/transcripts/262197031-tradingkey","https://www.businessessays.ai/business-strategy/a-competitor-analysis-of-nike-in-the-global-sportswear-market/","https://www.companieshistory.com/nike-competitors/","https://pestel-analysis.com/blogs/competitors/nike","https://businessmodelanalyst.com/nike-swot-analysis/","https://thedutchinvestors.substack.com/p/nike-premium-research-report-part","https://wwd.com/footwear-news/shoe-industry-news/nike-annual-shareholder-meeting-ceo-elliott-hill-progress-1239201228/","https://finance.yahoo.com/markets/stocks/articles/nike-elliott-hill-aims-squash-205335634.html","https://www.benzinga.com/markets/equities/26/06/60007561/nike-ceo-elliott-hill-admits-turnaround-is-taking-longer-than-expected-we-still-have-work-to-do","https://fortune.com/2026/07/04/nike-earnings-elliott-hill-nascent-comeback/","https://fortune.com/article/nike-ceo-veteran-elliott-hill-turnaround-growth/","https://www.spokesman.com/stories/2026/oct/04/nike-plans-more-job-cuts-to-boost-sputtering-turna/","https://bloomberg.com/opinion/articles/2026-10-05/nike-s-other-shoe-has-finally-dropped","https://www.forbes.com/sites/jimosman/2026/09/08/nike-stock-is-ripe-for-an-activist-but-theres-one-big-problem/"]}
